  "new_commit_message": "static-analysis: Scan files over 1 MB, and report the ones still skipped (#343)\n\nsemgrep skips any file larger than --max-target-bytes and reports nothing about\nit: no paths.skipped entry, no error, and the file simply absent from\npaths.scanned. run-scans.sh never set the flag, so semgrep's 1,000,000-byte\ndefault applied and a scan that never opened a file recorded `findings: 0` next\nto a plausible filesScanned count.\n\nReproduced on semgrep 1.178.0 against a 1,050,060-byte bundle carrying a\ndocument.write(location.hash) sink, beside a 36-byte sibling so the scan opened\nsomething and coveredNothing stayed empty:\n\n  before   findings 0, filesScanned 1, coveredNothing []\n  after    findings 1, filesScanned 2, maxTargetBytes 20000000\n\nThe limit is now explicit at 20,000,000 bytes and settable with\n--max-target-bytes N, where 0 means no limit as it does in semgrep. Bytes only:\nthe oversized report below compares the limit against a file size, and semgrep's\n'1.5MB' spelling would have to be parsed a second way that could disagree.\n\nA higher default moves the cliff rather than removing it, so the files still\nover it are named as `oversized` in scans.json, scoped to the --include globs a\nscan actually passed. The report phase of the workflow and the report template\ncarry it for the reason they carry coveredNothing: a gap only stderr knows about\nis a gap the report calls coverage. Cross-language rulesets take no --include and\nsemgrep decides for itself what it can parse, so their share is not enumerable\nhere; references/scan-modes.md says so and maxTargetBytes is in the report.\n\nEleven assertions added to tests/run_scan_tests.sh (104 in total), and one to\nthe workflow harness with the mutation that proves it load-bearing.\n\nRefs #298\n\nSigned-off-by: Udaya Tejas <udayatejas2004@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: kz-tob <kara.zaffarano@trailofbits.com>",
